.TH "RTCDTLSTRANSPORT.GETREMOTECERTIFICATES" "3JS" "July 13, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
RTCDtlsTransport.getRemoteCertificates \- RTCDtlsTransport:
getRemoteCertificates() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]getRemoteCertificates()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]RTCDtlsTransport\f[R] interface returns the certificate chain of
the remote peer of the DTLS connection.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
getRemoteCertificates()
.EE
.SS Parameters
None.
.SS Return value
An array of \f[CR]ArrayBuffer\f[R] objects that represent the remote
peer\(cqs certificate chain.
Each object contains a DER\-encoded X.509 certificate.
.PP
For \f[CR]new\f[R] connections, this is an empty array.
It is populated with the certificates from the remote peer when the
state of the transport changes to \f[CR]connected\f[R].
.SS Exceptions
None
.SH DESCRIPTION
WebRTC media and data channels are secured using Datagram Transport
Layer Security (DTLS).
During signaling, each endpoint advertises the fingerprint of the DTLS
certificate it will present.
During the DTLS handshake, the browser verifies that the certificate
presented by the remote peer matches the fingerprint in the negotiated
SDP.
.PP
DTLS guarantees the connected peer is the one you\(cqve been negotiating
with, because only that peer has the private key matching the
certificate whose fingerprint was exchanged during signaling.
However, since WebRTC uses self\-signed certificates rather than those
issued by a certificate authority, the certificate does not identify the
person, service, or device at the other end.
Establishing the identity of the remote peer usually requires an
out\-of\-band mechanism such as comparing certificate fingerprints
verbally over a phone call, or using a separate authenticated channel.
.PP
The \f[CR]getRemoteCertificates()\f[R] method allows you to get the
remote certificates used by DTLS and use them for
\f[I]application\-layer\f[R] authentication of the remote peer.
.PP
For fingerprint continuity, each peer must use the same certificate
across sessions, rather than generating a new one each time they
connect.
After connecting to the remote peer, you\(cqd exchange information
out\-of\-band to verify that it is the intended party, and use
\f[CR]getRemoteCertificates()\f[R] to get its certificates.
When you subsequently connect to that remote peer, you\(cqd only allow
communication if it has exactly the same certificate fingerprints.
There is still a window for a person\-in\-the\-middle attack, but it
only exists for the very first connection between peers.
.PP
Note that \f[CR]getRemoteCertificates()\f[R] returns raw DER\-encoded
X.509 certificates as \f[CR]ArrayBuffer\f[R] objects.
Distinguished Encoding Rules (DER) is the binary serialization format
used for X.509 certificates in TLS and DTLS.
Unlike \f[CR]RTCCertificate\f[R], these buffers do not expose
fingerprints and expiration dates directly.
To work with them you must process the raw bytes: you can hash the
buffer with \f[CR]SubtleCrypto.digest()\f[R] to compute a fingerprint
(as shown in the example below) or pass it to an X.509 parsing library
to inspect individual fields.
.SH EXAMPLE
.SS Basic usage
This example shows how to fingerprint the remote peer\(cqs certificate
after the DTLS handshake and compare it against a stored value.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]async\f[R] \f[B]function\f[R] getRemoteFingerprint(dtlsTransport) {
  \f[B]const\f[R] certs = dtlsTransport.getRemoteCertificates();
  \f[B]if\f[R] (certs.length === 0) {
    \f[B]return\f[R] \f[B]null\f[R];
  }

  \f[I]// Hash the raw DER\-encoded certificate with SHA\-256\f[R]
  \f[B]const\f[R] digest = \f[B]await\f[R] crypto.subtle.digest(\(dqSHA\-256\(dq, certs[0]);

  \f[I]// Format as colon\-separated hex, matching the SDP a=fingerprint convention\f[R]
  \f[B]return\f[R] Array.from(\f[B]new\f[R] Uint8Array(digest))
    .map((b) \f[B]=>\f[R] b.toString(16).padStart(2, \(dq0\(dq))
    .join(\(dq:\(dq);
}

\f[I]// Call after the DTLS handshake is complete\f[R]
pc.addEventListener(\(dqconnectionstatechange\(dq, \f[B]async\f[R] () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  \f[B]if\f[R] (pc.connectionState === \(dqconnected\(dq) {
    \f[B]const\f[R] sender = pc.getSenders()[0];
    \f[B]if\f[R] (!sender?.transport) \f[B]return\f[R];

    \f[B]const\f[R] fingerprint = \f[B]await\f[R] getRemoteFingerprint(sender.transport);
    \f[B]if\f[R] (!fingerprint) \f[B]return\f[R];
    \f[B]const\f[R] stored = localStorage.getItem(\(dqremote\-peer\-fingerprint\(dq);

    \f[B]if\f[R] (!stored) {
      localStorage.setItem(\(dqremote\-peer\-fingerprint\(dq, fingerprint);
    } \f[B]else\f[R] \f[B]if\f[R] (stored !== fingerprint) {
      console.error(\(dqCertificate mismatch \(em closing connection\(dq);
      pc.close();
    }
  }
});
.EE
